    $astroGP_master$> There is a line drawn across the young solar system, a little past two and a half times the Earth's distance from the Sun, and it divides everything. Inside it, worlds were built from the scraps of rock and metal that could survive the heat — small, dense, slow to grow, and finished with whatever solid crumbs the disk left them. Outside it, water froze, the amount of solid material available to build with jumped several-fold, and a handful of cores grew fast enough to reach a critical size before the gas ran out. Those cores did not settle for crumbs. They seized the disk itself, and became the giant planets — objects so large that they, not the terrestrial worlds, are the real content of the solar system. This chapter makes one argument: that a single circumstance, the freezing-out of ice beyond the snow line racing against the clock of a dissipating gas disk, produced the giants, and that having produced them it dictated everything else — how much of the planetary mass ended up in two bodies, why there are two utterly different kinds of giant, why Mars is a runt, why the asteroid belt is grooved, where Earth's water may have come from, and whether the largest planet is our shield or our hazard. To understand the solar system you must first understand that it is a giant-planet system with a few rocks near the fire.

## The line that makes giants

Every model of how the Sun's planets formed begins with the same object: a flattened, rotating disk of gas and dust left over from the Sun's own birth, a few percent of a solar mass spread across tens of astronomical units. The gas is overwhelmingly hydrogen and helium, in essentially solar proportions. The dust — the roughly one to two percent that is not hydrogen and helium — is the raw material of planets. And the single most important fact about that dust is that whether a given compound is solid or gaseous depends on how far it is from the Sun.

Temperature in the disk falls with distance. To a first approximation the equilibrium temperature of a grain goes as

$$T(r) \approx 278 \ \text{K} \left(\frac{r}{1\ \text{AU}}\right)^{-1/2},$$

so a grain at 1 AU sits near 280 K, and one at 5 AU near 125 K. Different compounds condense at different temperatures. Silicates and iron condense hot, close in, which is why the inner disk could only ever build worlds out of rock and metal. Water is different, and water is abundant. Oxygen is the third most common element in the cosmos, and where it is cold enough — below roughly 150 to 170 K — water freezes onto grains as ice. The distance at which this happens is the **snow line**, and in the early solar system it sat at about **2.7 AU**, out in what is now the asteroid belt.

{{image: Protoplanetary disk | The disk of gas and dust around a young star, the birthplace of planets. Temperature falls outward, and where it drops below about 170 K water freezes onto grains, abruptly multiplying the solid material available to build cores. That radius — the snow line — is the single most consequential boundary in a planetary system.}}

Cross that line and something abrupt happens to the bookkeeping. Water is made of two of the most abundant elements there are, and in the outer disk there is a great deal of it. When it freezes, the mass of solid material available for building — the surface density of solids, the kilograms of buildable stuff per square metre of disk — **jumps by a factor of two to four** essentially at a single radius. Inside the line you build planets from rock and metal alone, perhaps 0.3 percent of the disk's mass by the local reckoning. Outside it you build from rock and metal *and ice*, and there is suddenly two to four times as much to work with.

That factor of a few does not sound like the difference between a Mars and a Jupiter. The reason it becomes exactly that is a race against a clock.

### Core growth against the disk clock

The outer solar system builds planets the same way the inner one does, from the bottom up: dust sticks into pebbles, pebbles into planetesimals, planetesimals sweep up their neighbours and grow into cores. The rate at which a core grows depends steeply on how much solid material is lying around to be swept up. More surface density means faster growth and a larger final body before the core runs out of nearby material — the so-called isolation mass scales roughly as the surface density of solids to the three-halves power. Multiply the available solids by a factor of a few and you multiply the mass a core can reach by considerably more. Beyond the snow line, cores can grow to something like ten Earth masses. Inside it, starved of ice, they stall at a fraction of that — which is precisely the size of the terrestrial planets and the largest asteroids.

Ten Earth masses is not an arbitrary figure. It is, to order of magnitude, the **critical core mass**: the point at which a solid core's gravity is strong enough that the gas it has been quietly gathering around itself can no longer support its own weight against contraction. Below the critical mass a core wears a thin, static atmosphere in hydrostatic balance. Reach it, and that balance fails. The envelope begins to contract, contraction lets more gas fall in, more gas means more weight and faster contraction, and the process runs away. This is **runaway gas accretion**, and once it starts a planet gorges, pulling in hundreds of Earth masses of hydrogen and helium straight from the disk in a geological instant — as little as tens of thousands to a hundred thousand years — until it either exhausts its local supply or carves a gap in the disk and starves.

Now the clock. The gas disk does not last. Ultraviolet light from the young Sun and from nearby hot stars heats and boils it away; the star accretes some of it; radiation pressure and winds sweep out the rest. Observations of young stellar clusters show that the gas is essentially gone within **a few million years** — call it two to ten, with most stars losing their disks by around three. This is the whole story, compressed into a single sentence: **a core must reach the critical ten Earth masses before the gas disk evaporates, or it never becomes a giant.** Beyond the snow line, with two to four times the solids, cores win that race. Inside it they lose. And a core that reaches critical mass a little too late — after the gas has begun to thin but before it is entirely gone — captures some gas but never enough to run away. Hold that last case in mind. It is the origin of an entire second class of planet, and we will return to it.

So the giants exist because of an accident of timing that the snow line arranges. The line is where ice condenses; ice is what lets cores grow fast; fast growth is what beats the disk clock; and beating the clock is what turns a ten-Earth-mass rock-and-ice core into a three-hundred-Earth-mass ball of captured hydrogen. Everything that follows in this chapter is downstream of that race.

## The scale correction

Here is where the argument demands that you throw out a picture you were given in childhood and install a truer one.

The schoolroom solar system is eight planets in a tidy row, beads on a wire, Mercury to Neptune, with Earth a comfortable third — a member of a family. It is one of the most quietly misleading images in all of science education, because it gets the single most important quantity, mass, wrong by more than an order of magnitude. Look at what the giants actually weigh, in Earth masses:

| Body | Mass (Earth = 1) |
|---|---|
| **Jupiter** | **317.8** |
| Saturn | 95.2 |
| Neptune | 17.1 |
| Uranus | 14.5 |
| Earth | 1.00 |
| Venus | 0.815 |
| Mars | 0.107 |
| Mercury | 0.055 |

Add every planet that is not Jupiter — Saturn, both ice giants, all four terrestrials — and you get 128.8 Earth masses. **Jupiter alone outweighs that total by a factor of 2.47.** One planet is nearly two and a half times heavier than every other planet in the solar system put together.

Put the two gas giants together and Jupiter plus Saturn come to 413.0 Earth masses out of a planetary total of 446.6 — which is to say **92.5 percent of all the planetary mass in the solar system lives in two bodies.** Jupiter by itself is 71.2 percent. Everything else — Saturn's smaller sibling ice giants, all four rocky worlds, every moon, every asteroid, the entire Kuiper belt, all the comets there have ever been — is the remaining **7.5 percent**, a garnish.

{{image: Jupiter | Jupiter, which by itself holds 71 percent of all the mass in the Sun's planetary system — nearly two and a half times every other planet combined. The correct mental picture of the solar system is not eight equal beads on a wire but a star, then this, then a long tail of debris.}}

The mass is not the only place the giants dominate. Count the moons. The four giant planets have swept up and retained satellite systems of extraordinary richness — Jupiter and Saturn each command well over a hundred confirmed moons, and Uranus and Neptune dozens more — while the four terrestrial worlds have exactly three moons between them, two of which are captured asteroids barely worth the name. Of every moon known to orbit a planet in this solar system, **on the order of 99 percent orbit one of the four giants.** The giants did not merely take most of the planetary mass; they took nearly all of the planetary *retinue* too, because a body massive enough to seize a slab of the gas disk is also massive enough to hang on to the debris that wanders near it.

So replace the beads. **The solar system is the Sun, then Jupiter, then a long tail of debris — and we live in the debris.** This is not rhetorical deflation for its own sake. It is the correct scaling, and it reorganises every question you might ask. When you wonder why the inner planets are arranged as they are, or where their water came from, or why the asteroid belt failed to assemble into a planet, the honest first move is to ask what the largest object in the region — always a giant — was doing. Earth is not a typical planet whose neighbours are minor perturbations. Earth is a 0.2-percent contaminant in a system built and stirred by objects three hundred times its mass. The rest of this chapter is what happens when you take that seriously.

## Two kinds of giant, two fossils of formation

The four giants are not one family but two, and the split is not a matter of degree. It is a matter of composition, and composition here is a fossil — a direct record of *where and when* each planet formed relative to the snow line and the disk clock.

**Jupiter and Saturn are gas giants.** Their bulk composition is close to the Sun's: hydrogen and helium in nearly solar proportions, with a modest enrichment in heavy elements concentrated toward a diffuse deep core. They are, to first approximation, balls of captured nebular gas with a rock-and-ice seed buried and smeared at the centre. That is exactly what the runaway-accretion story predicts for a core that reaches critical mass *early*, while the disk is still fat with gas. Reach ten Earth masses at two or three million years, with plenty of hydrogen still around, and you gorge. Jupiter, forming just outside the snow line where solids were densest and growth fastest, won the race by the largest margin and grew the largest. Saturn, a little farther out and a little later, won it by less and grew to under a third of Jupiter's mass. Both, crucially, *won it* — they reached runaway while abundant gas remained, and so both are made mostly of that gas.

**Uranus and Neptune are ice giants, and they are a different kind of object entirely.** Their masses are far smaller — 14.5 and 17.1 Earth masses, close to the critical core mass itself — and their composition tells you why. Beneath a relatively thin skin of hydrogen and helium (perhaps one to a few Earth masses of gas, a genuine atmosphere rather than the bulk of the planet), the ice giants are dominated by a hot, dense mantle of what planetary scientists loosely call "ices": water, ammonia, and methane, the frozen volatiles that condensed in the cold outer disk. They are not failed gas giants that happen to be smaller. They are worlds that formed farther out, where the disk was thinner and colder and orbital times were longer, so that core growth was slower — and their cores crossed the critical ten-Earth-mass threshold *too late*, after the gas had largely dispersed. They began to accrete gas, and then the gas was gone before runaway could carry them away. Their thin hydrogen envelopes are the little they managed to grab before the disk clock ran out.

Read the compositions side by side and the whole formation sequence is written in them. A gas giant's near-solar hydrogen-helium bulk says *I reached runaway while the disk was full.* An ice giant's ice-dominated mantle under a hydrogen veneer says *I reached the threshold after the gas had thinned; I caught only a whiff.* Same physics — core accretion racing a dissipating disk — with the outcome hinging entirely on the timing, and the timing set by where in the disk each core was trying to grow. Composition is the fossil, and *where and when* is what it records.

This split is the hinge of the whole unit. The two chapters that follow develop the two branches: the gas giants, with their metallic-hydrogen interiors, planet-scale magnetic fields, and internal heat that drives weather from below; and the ice giants, with their exotic ice mantles, tipped and offset magnetic fields, and the deep puzzle of why the two supposedly similar worlds are so unalike. The point to carry forward from here is that the difference between them is not cosmetic. It is a difference in *when the race was won*, frozen into chemistry four and a half billion years ago.

## What having a giant does to everything else

A giant planet is not a passive occupant of its orbit. It is a gravitational engine that reaches across the entire system, and the inner planets — including the one you are standing on — were shaped as much by Jupiter's presence as by their own local physics. Three consequences deserve to be spelled out with numbers, and then one popular claim deserves to be handled honestly rather than repeated.

### The Grand Tack, and why Mars is a runt

There is a longstanding embarrassment in the inner solar system: **Mars is too small.** Simulations that grow the terrestrial planets from a smooth disk of rocky material stretching from Venus's orbit out past Mars reliably produce a Mars two to ten times heavier than the real one — an Earth-sized or at least Venus-sized planet where we find a body one-ninth of Earth's mass. Something removed most of the material that should have built Mars, and left a sharp outer edge to the terrestrial region near 1 AU.

The leading explanation puts the blame squarely on Jupiter. In the **Grand Tack** scenario, Jupiter formed near 3.5 AU and then, still embedded in the gas disk, migrated *inward* — spiralling all the way to about 1.5 AU, roughly the present orbit of Mars — before reversing course and migrating back out to near 5 AU. The reversal (the "tack," borrowed from sailing) happens when Saturn forms, migrates inward faster, catches Jupiter, and the two lock into a resonance that flips the direction of their interaction with the gas. As Jupiter plunged in and then retreated, it acted like a snowplough, shepherding and scattering the planetesimals of the inner disk and **truncating the disk of solids at about 1 AU.** Mars was left to form in a starved, swept region with little to accrete — hence its arrested growth. Earth and Venus, forming in the dense surviving material inside the truncation, grew to full size.

{{image: Grand tack hypothesis | The Grand Tack: Jupiter migrating inward through the young disk to about 1.5 AU and then back out, truncating the belt of rocky material near 1 AU. Starving the region where Mars was forming, it offers an explanation for why Mars grew to only a ninth of Earth's mass — and it mixed the asteroid belt in passing.}}

The same passage did something else on its way through: it raked the region that is now the asteroid belt, scattering some bodies inward and outward, depleting the belt of most of its original mass, and mixing two populations that had formed on opposite sides of the snow line — dry, rocky bodies from inside and water-rich, icy bodies from outside — into the compositionally stratified belt we see today. The Grand Tack is not universally accepted; it is one hypothesis among several, and it makes assumptions about the timing of Saturn's growth that are hard to test. But it captures the essential point with unusual clarity: **the size of Mars and the emptiness of the asteroid belt may both be side effects of where Jupiter chose to wander.**

### Sculpting at a distance: the Kirkwood gaps

You do not need a giant to migrate through a region to see it reshape that region. Even sitting still, a giant sculpts structure across tens of millions of kilometres through **orbital resonance**.

Plot the asteroids of the main belt by their distance from the Sun and the distribution is not smooth. It is grooved — swept clean at specific radii, the **Kirkwood gaps**, discovered by Daniel Kirkwood in 1866. Each gap sits at a distance where an asteroid's orbital period is a simple whole-number ratio of Jupiter's. At the 3:1 gap an asteroid completes exactly three orbits for each one of Jupiter's; at the 5:2 gap, five for every two; and there are gaps at 7:3 and 2:1 as well. Kepler's third law puts these resonances at definite radii. For the 3:1 resonance,

$$a = a_J \left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^{2/3} = 5.20\ \text{AU} \times 0.481 = 2.50\ \text{AU},$$

and the arithmetic lands each gap exactly where the belt is empty.

Why does a resonance clear a gap? Because at a mean-motion resonance the little tugs Jupiter gives the asteroid on each pass all come at the same phase of its orbit and add up instead of averaging away. Over thousands of orbits these coherent kicks pump the asteroid's eccentricity up until its stretched orbit crosses the path of Mars or Earth or plunges toward the Sun, and it is removed — flung out, dropped in, or made into an inner-solar-system impactor. The gap is not a place where asteroids cannot go; it is a place from which they are relentlessly evicted. It is the clearest demonstration in the solar system that a giant carves structure into a population it never touches, purely through the patient arithmetic of gravity and time.

{{image: Kirkwood gap | The Kirkwood gaps: the main asteroid belt is swept clean at the distances where an asteroid's orbital period forms a simple whole-number ratio with Jupiter's — 3:1, 5:2, 7:3, 2:1. A giant carves structure across tens of millions of kilometres of empty space through resonance alone.}}

The same resonant machinery works in reverse to build structure as well as destroy it. Beyond the belt, whole families of bodies are *shepherded* into resonances rather than expelled from them — the Trojan swarms locked sixty degrees ahead of and behind Jupiter at its triangular Lagrange points, and, far out in the Kuiper belt, Pluto and its kin held in a 3:2 resonance that keeps them from ever colliding with Neptune. Resonance is the fundamental way a giant imposes order on everything sharing its neighbourhood.

### Delivery: where the inner planets got their volatiles

There is a paradox about Earth. It formed inside the snow line, out of dry rock, in a region too hot for water ice to condense — and yet it is a water world, its surface two-thirds ocean. The water had to be delivered from somewhere colder, from beyond the snow line where ice was abundant. And the delivery service was run by the giants.

A giant planet is a superb scatterer. Any icy planetesimal that strays near it gets a violent gravitational kick, and some fraction of those kicks are aimed inward, onto orbits that cross the inner solar system. Over the tens of millions of years while the giants were finishing their growth and settling their orbits, they flung a rain of water-bearing bodies — icy planetesimals, primitive asteroids from the outer belt, comets from farther still — toward the inner planets. Some of that material struck the young Earth and stayed. **The giants, in other words, may be the reason the inner planets are wet.** The isotopic details are still argued over — the ratio of deuterium to ordinary hydrogen in Earth's oceans matches certain classes of asteroid better than it matches most comets, which points more to the outer asteroid belt than to the deep-freeze comets as the main source — but the delivery mechanism in every version is the same: a giant's gravity throwing volatiles down the well toward the rocks near the fire.

That same scattering, extended and intensified, is what the **Nice model** describes for a later epoch. In it, a slow migration of the giants a few hundred million years after formation carried them across a mutual resonance, briefly destabilising the whole outer system, scattering the remaining planetesimal disk in every direction and triggering a spike of impacts across the inner solar system — a candidate explanation for the ages of the great lunar basins. Whatever the details, the lesson is consistent with everything else in this chapter: the giants are the stirring rods of the solar system, and the volatiles that make the inner planets habitable were very likely stirred inward by them.

{{image: Nice model | The Nice model: a later migration of the giant planets across a mutual resonance destabilises the outer disk, scattering icy bodies throughout the solar system. The same scattering that may have delivered volatiles to the inner planets also, in this picture, drove a spike of impacts recorded in the ages of the lunar basins.}}

### The shield that is also a slingshot

And so to the claim you have certainly heard: that Jupiter is Earth's shield, a benevolent giant standing guard at the edge of the inner solar system, catching the comets and asteroids that would otherwise have sterilised us, so that complex life had the billions of undisturbed years it needed. It is a lovely story. It is repeated in popular science as though it were established fact. And it is, at best, half true — which means that repeating it uncritically is a failure of exactly the honesty this book is trying to teach.

The comforting version rests on a real phenomenon. Jupiter genuinely does deflect and eject some bodies that would otherwise reach the inner planets, and it has spectacularly consumed at least one comet in living memory: in July 1994 the fragments of Comet Shoemaker–Levy 9 slammed into Jupiter one after another, leaving Earth-sized bruises in its clouds, a body that Jupiter's gravity had captured and torn apart rather than let wander free. Jupiter clears its neighbourhood. Some of what it clears would, in its absence, have come our way.

But the same gravity that deflects also *injects*. Jupiter's resonances, the very Kirkwood gaps described above, are not only removal channels — they are the principal machinery that pumps asteroids out of the belt and onto Earth-crossing orbits in the first place. A giant that can fling an icy body inward to deliver water can equally fling a rocky body inward to deliver a catastrophe; the physics does not distinguish the outcomes. When the dynamicists actually ran the experiment — most systematically in a series of simulations by Horner and Jones, who rebuilt the solar system with giant planets of varying mass and simply counted how many impactors reached Earth over long integrations — the reassuring picture did not survive. They found that Jupiter both shields Earth and supplies it, and that the balance between the two is delicate and non-monotonic. A Jupiter-mass planet is **not** cleanly protective. Remove the giant entirely and the impact flux from some populations actually *drops*, because you have removed the scatterer that feeds them inward. Most strikingly, an **intermediate-mass** giant — smaller than Jupiter, roughly in the range between a large ice giant and a modest gas giant — can be the *worst* case of all: massive enough to perturb the small-body reservoirs vigorously and hurl their contents inward, but not massive enough to efficiently eject that material from the solar system afterward. The full-mass Jupiter we happen to have is, if anything, better at ejecting than a smaller giant would be, but it is nobody's clean guardian.

The honest summary is this. Jupiter is a shield *and* a slingshot, and which effect dominates depends on the population of impactors, the mass of the giant, and the epoch you look at. It saves us from some things and sends us others, and the net balance is close enough to even that serious simulations disagree about its sign. The tidy fable — big friendly Jupiter sweeping the skies clean so that Earth could flourish — is not something the dynamics support, and a textbook that argues a thesis has an obligation not to pass it along. What having a giant *does* to everything else is real, enormous, and double-edged. It is not a guardian angel. It is a force of nature, indifferent to whether the rock it redirects lands on us or misses.

## Pulling the thread

The whole chapter descends from one line and one clock. Beyond about 2.7 AU water ice condensed, the surface density of solids jumped two-to-fourfold, and cores grew fast enough to reach the critical ten Earth masses before the gas disk evaporated in a few million years. That single circumstance made the giants, and having made them it made everything else:

- Because the race was won *beyond* the snow line and lost inside it, nearly all the planetary mass — 92.5 percent in two bodies, 71 percent in Jupiter alone — and nearly all the moons ended up out there. The solar system is a giant-planet system with a few rocks near the fire.
- Because the outcome hinged on *timing*, there are two kinds of giant: gas giants that reached runaway while the disk was full and kept their solar hydrogen, and ice giants that crossed the threshold too late and kept only ices under a hydrogen skin. Composition is a fossil of where and when.
- Because a giant is a gravitational engine, it reached back into the inner solar system and reshaped it — starving Mars in the Grand Tack, grooving the belt at its resonances, scattering the volatiles that may have filled Earth's oceans, and, far from simply guarding us, both deflecting and injecting the bodies that cross our path.

Take the giants seriously and the inner solar system stops looking like a self-contained family of rocky worlds and starts looking like what it is: the debris field of a two-body system, sculpted from a distance by the objects that won the race at the snow line. The chapters that follow take the two branches of that race in turn.

## Further reading

The primary science here lives in the research literature on planet formation and solar-system dynamics, much of it freely readable in preprint form. For the snow line and core-accretion timing, any modern review of giant-planet formation lays out the critical-core-mass argument and the disk-lifetime constraint. The Grand Tack and the Nice model each have accessible founding papers (Walsh and collaborators for the former; Tsiganis, Gomes, Morbidelli and Levison for the latter) that are written to be understood. And the honest treatment of the "Jupiter as shield" question is best pursued through the Horner and Jones simulation papers themselves, which are unusually clear about what they did and did not find. For a single narrative that sets the giants at the centre of the solar system's history rather than at its edge, {{book: Michael Summers and James Trefil | Exoplanets: Diamond Worlds, Super Earths, Pulsar Planets, and the New Search for Life beyond Our Solar System | 2017}} places our giant-planet architecture in the wider context of the thousands of planetary systems now known — many of which are arranged nothing like ours.

- **Summers, Michael and Trefil, James, *Exoplanets* (2017).** A readable tour of how strange our giant-planet arrangement looks once you can compare it with the exoplanet catalogue, and why that comparison matters for the questions this chapter opens.

## Problems

*Constants and data you may need: $G = 6.674\times10^{-11}\ \text{N·m}^2/\text{kg}^2$; $M_\odot = 1.989\times10^{30}$ kg; $M_\oplus = 5.972\times10^{24}$ kg. Jupiter: mass $317.8\,M_\oplus$, semi-major axis $a_J = 5.204$ AU. Saturn $95.2\,M_\oplus$, Neptune $17.1\,M_\oplus$, Uranus $14.5\,M_\oplus$, Venus $0.815\,M_\oplus$, Mars $0.107\,M_\oplus$, Mercury $0.055\,M_\oplus$. Disk temperature law $T(r) = 278\ \text{K}\,(r/\text{AU})^{-1/2}$. Problem 1 (the scale correction) and problem 4 (the timing race) are the conceptual core; problem 7 has no settled answer and is meant to be argued.*

**1 — Installing the right picture.** **(a)** Using the mass table, compute the total planetary mass in Earth masses, then Jupiter's share of it as a percentage, and Jupiter-plus-Saturn's share. **(b)** By what factor does Jupiter alone outweigh every other planet combined? **(c)** In one sentence, state the mental picture these numbers should replace, and what should replace it. **(d)** Roughly 99 percent of the solar system's moons orbit the four giants. Explain, in terms of the argument of this chapter, why the same objects should dominate both the mass budget *and* the satellite count — why these are not two independent facts.

**2 — Locating the snow line.** **(a)** Using the disk temperature law, find the distance at which the temperature equals 170 K, the condensation temperature of water ice. **(b)** Repeat for 150 K. **(c)** The surface density of solids jumps by a factor of two to four across this line. Explain in words why water in particular — rather than, say, iron — is responsible for so large a jump, referring to the cosmic abundance of the elements involved. **(d)** Why does the *location* of the snow line move inward over the first few million years of the disk's life, and what does that complicate about the simple picture?

**3 — Carving the Kirkwood gaps.** **(a)** Using Kepler's third law, compute the semi-major axes of the 3:1, 5:2, 7:3, and 2:1 mean-motion resonances with Jupiter, taking $a_J = 5.204$ AU. (For a p:q interior resonance where the asteroid completes $p$ orbits per $q$ of Jupiter's, $a = a_J\,(q/p)^{2/3}$.) **(b)** These are locations where the belt is *empty*, not crowded. Explain the mechanism: why do coherent, repeated tugs at a resonance clear a gap rather than trap objects in it? **(c)** Yet the Trojans are *trapped* in a 1:1 co-orbital resonance, and Pluto is *held* in a 3:2 with Neptune. Reconcile this with (b): why does resonance sometimes eject and sometimes protect?

**4 — The race the giants won.** A core beyond the snow line must reach the critical mass of about $10\,M_\oplus$ before the gas disk dissipates in a few million years. **(a)** State the two competing timescales explicitly and explain which physical quantity, boosted by the snow line, tips the race in the outer disk's favour. **(b)** The isolation mass of a core scales roughly as the surface density of solids to the power $3/2$. If crossing the snow line multiplies that surface density by 3, by what factor does the isolation mass increase? **(c)** A core that reaches critical mass *early*, while gas is abundant, becomes a gas giant; one that reaches it *late*, as gas is dispersing, becomes an ice giant. Use this to explain why Uranus and Neptune have masses close to the critical core mass itself, with only thin hydrogen envelopes. **(d)** What single change to the disk — same everything else — would have turned Jupiter into an ice giant?

**5 — Composition as a fossil.** **(a)** Jupiter and Saturn have bulk compositions close to solar (mostly hydrogen and helium); Uranus and Neptune are dominated by water, ammonia, and methane ices under thin hydrogen skins. Explain how each composition records *when* the planet reached critical core mass relative to the disk's lifetime. **(b)** Why is "ice giant" a better name than "small gas giant" for Uranus and Neptune — what does the alternative name get wrong about their history? **(c)** Suppose you found an exoplanet the mass of Saturn but with the ice-dominated composition of Neptune. What would that tell you about the disk it formed in?

**6 — Starving Mars.** **(a)** Standard simulations that grow the terrestrial planets from a smooth disk overproduce Mars by a large factor. State the problem quantitatively (Mars's actual mass in Earth masses, versus what smooth-disk models predict). **(b)** Explain how the Grand Tack resolves it, and identify the specific feature of the inner disk that Jupiter's excursion is invoked to create. **(c)** The same excursion is said to explain the low mass and mixed composition of the asteroid belt. Explain both effects in one or two sentences each. **(d)** The Grand Tack is a hypothesis, not established fact. Name one assumption it makes that is hard to test, and explain why that matters for how much weight to put on it.

**7 — Open problem: shield, slingshot, or neither.** The popular claim that Jupiter is "Earth's shield" is, at best, half true. **(a)** Give the strongest version of the case *for* Jupiter as protector, and then the strongest version of the case that Jupiter is a net *hazard*, each in terms of specific dynamical mechanisms from this chapter. **(b)** Horner and Jones found that an intermediate-mass giant can be worse for Earth than either a full Jupiter or no giant at all. Explain the physical reason a middling giant could maximise the impact flux. **(c)** Design, in outline, the simulation you would run to settle whether *our actual* Jupiter is net-protective for *our actual* Earth over the last four billion years. What would you vary, what would you measure, and what result would count as an answer? **(d)** Step back: given the exoplanet catalogue shows that giant-planet architectures like ours are far from universal, argue one side of the question of whether a Jupiter is a *prerequisite* for a habitable inner planet, a *hazard* to one, or simply *irrelevant*. There is no settled answer; make the best case you can and state what evidence would change your mind.

## Worked answers

**1 — Installing the right picture.** **(a)** Summing the table: $317.8 + 95.2 + 17.1 + 14.5 + 1.00 + 0.815 + 0.107 + 0.055 = 446.6\,M_\oplus$. Jupiter's share: $317.8/446.6 = \mathbf{71.2\%}$. Jupiter plus Saturn: $413.0/446.6 = \mathbf{92.5\%}$. **(b)** Everything that is not Jupiter totals $446.6 - 317.8 = 128.8\,M_\oplus$, so Jupiter outweighs all other planets combined by $317.8/128.8 = \mathbf{2.47\times}$. **(c)** Replace *"the Sun and its eight planets, arranged in a row like equal beads"* with **"the Sun, then Jupiter, then a long tail of debris — and we live in the debris."** **(d)** They are the same fact seen twice. A body's ability to grab the disk's gas and its ability to retain a swarm of satellites both scale with mass: only a planet massive enough to win the runaway-accretion race is massive enough to hold a deep gravitational well, and a deep well captures and keeps both a large gas envelope *and* a large retinue of moons. Mass dominance and moon dominance are two readings of the same underlying quantity — gravitational reach — so the giants necessarily top both lists at once.

**2 — Locating the snow line.** **(a)** Set $170 = 278\,(r/\text{AU})^{-1/2}$. Then $(r/\text{AU})^{-1/2} = 170/278 = 0.6115$, so $(r/\text{AU})^{1/2} = 1.635$ and $r = 1.635^2 = \mathbf{2.67\ AU}$. **(b)** For 150 K: $150/278 = 0.5396$, $(r/\text{AU})^{1/2} = 1.853$, $r = \mathbf{3.44\ AU}$. So even this crude law puts the snow line in the region of the asteroid belt, and the exact figure depends on the assumed condensation temperature. **(c)** Water is built from hydrogen (the most abundant element) and oxygen (the third most abundant), so there is a great deal of it available to freeze; when it condenses it adds a large mass of solid per unit disk area. Iron, by contrast, is far less abundant and condenses much closer to the Sun; its freeze-out adds comparatively little solid mass and does so where planet-building is already rock-limited. The snow line jump is large *because the compound crossing it is made of the cosmos's most common atoms.* **(d)** As the disk ages, accretion slows and the disk cools, so the radius at which the temperature drops to the ice-condensation value moves *inward* over time. This complicates the "single fixed line" picture: the boundary is a moving target, so exactly which planetesimals had access to ice depends on *when* they were assembling, not just where — adding a second time dimension to an already timing-dominated story.

**3 — Carving the Kirkwood gaps.** **(a)** Using $a = a_J\,(q/p)^{2/3}$ with $a_J = 5.204$ AU:
- 3:1 → $(1/3)^{2/3} = 0.4807$ → $a = \mathbf{2.50\ AU}$
- 5:2 → $(2/5)^{2/3} = 0.5429$ → $a = \mathbf{2.82\ AU}$
- 7:3 → $(3/7)^{2/3} = 0.5714$ → $a = \mathbf{2.97\ AU}$
- 2:1 → $(1/2)^{2/3} = 0.6300$ → $a = \mathbf{3.28\ AU}$

Each lands where the observed belt shows a gap. **(b)** At a mean-motion resonance the asteroid meets Jupiter at the same phase of its orbit every cycle, so Jupiter's small gravitational tugs arrive coherently and *add* rather than averaging to zero. The accumulated kicks pump the asteroid's orbital eccentricity higher and higher until its orbit crosses that of Mars or Earth, or dives toward the Sun, and the body is removed by a close encounter or collision. The resonance is therefore an *escape hatch*, not a trap, for these bodies — hence a gap. **(c)** Whether a resonance ejects or protects depends on its geometry. The Kirkwood resonances drive up eccentricity while allowing the asteroid to make close approaches to Jupiter, which destabilises it. The Trojan (1:1) and Pluto's (3:2) resonances instead *librate* in a way that guarantees the body is always far from the perturber at conjunction — the 3:2 ensures Pluto is near aphelion, well away from Neptune, whenever the two line up. A protective resonance uses the same coherent tugging to *keep the bodies apart*; a clearing resonance uses it to *drive them together*. The sign depends on the phase relationship the resonance enforces.

**4 — The race the giants won.** **(a)** The two clocks are the *core-growth time* (how long to reach $\sim10\,M_\oplus$) and the *disk-dissipation time* (a few million years). Beyond the snow line, the surface density of solids is two-to-four times higher, which shortens the core-growth time — cores sweep up material faster and reach critical mass sooner. The giant forms only if growth beats dissipation, and the snow line tips that race by boosting solid surface density. **(b)** Isolation mass $\propto \Sigma^{3/2}$, so tripling $\Sigma$ multiplies it by $3^{3/2} = \mathbf{5.2\times}$. A factor of three in raw material becomes a factor of five in the mass a core can reach — which is how a modest density jump translates into the difference between a stalled rock and a critical-mass core. **(c)** An ice giant is a core that reached critical mass *just* as the gas was disappearing: it began runaway too late and captured only a thin envelope before the supply vanished. That is exactly why Uranus and Neptune sit near the critical mass ($14.5$ and $17.1\,M_\oplus$, close to the $\sim10\,M_\oplus$ threshold) with only one-to-a-few Earth masses of hydrogen on top — they are essentially the core plus whatever gas they grabbed in the disk's dying moments. **(d)** Form Jupiter's core *later* — or equivalently, give the disk a much shorter gas lifetime — so that the core crosses critical mass only as the gas is dispersing. Same core, same location, but it would catch only a thin envelope and end up an ice giant. The variable that decides gas-giant versus ice-giant is the timing of critical-mass crossing relative to gas dispersal, not the location or the final core mass alone.

**5 — Composition as a fossil.** **(a)** A near-solar hydrogen-helium bulk (Jupiter, Saturn) can only be acquired by capturing a large mass of nebular gas, which requires reaching critical core mass *while the disk is still full of gas* — i.e. early. An ice-dominated interior under a thin hydrogen skin (Uranus, Neptune) records a core that reached critical mass *late*, after most of the gas had dispersed, so it kept its ice-and-rock building blocks but caught only a whiff of hydrogen. The proportion of captured gas to native ice is a clock reading. **(b)** "Small gas giant" implies Uranus and Neptune are the same kind of object as Jupiter, merely scaled down — that they simply grabbed less of the same gas. But they are *compositionally* different: they are mostly ice, not mostly hydrogen. The name "ice giant" records that their bulk is the condensed volatiles of the cold outer disk, and that they failed to reach runaway gas capture at all — a difference of formation history, not just of size. **(c)** A Saturn-mass planet with Neptune-like ice-dominated composition would tell you it formed in a disk whose gas dispersed *before* the core could run away, despite the core growing large — pointing to a short-lived or gas-poor disk (or formation far out where gas was thin), in which a big core accumulated without ever gaining access to abundant hydrogen.

**6 — Starving Mars.** **(a)** Mars's actual mass is $0.107\,M_\oplus$, about one-ninth of Earth's. Simulations that grow the terrestrials from a smooth disk of solids extending through Mars's zone typically produce a Mars-region planet several times heavier — often Earth- or Venus-sized, an overprediction by a factor of order five to ten. **(b)** In the Grand Tack, Jupiter migrates inward to about 1.5 AU and back out, and in doing so it clears and truncates the disk of solids at about 1 AU. Mars then forms in a starved, swept-out region with little to accrete, so it stalls at low mass, while Earth and Venus form in the dense surviving material inside the truncation. The specific feature invoked is a *sharp outer edge to the terrestrial solid disk near 1 AU.* **(c)** *Low mass:* Jupiter's passage scattered most of the primordial asteroid-belt material out of the region, leaving the belt with a small fraction of its original mass. *Mixed composition:* the excursion swept together dry inner bodies and icy outer bodies into overlapping orbits, producing the belt's observed radial mixing of rocky and water-rich types. **(d)** It assumes Saturn grew to near its final mass fast enough to catch Jupiter and trigger the "tack" at the right moment — a timing constraint on giant-planet growth that we cannot directly observe and that competing formation models do not all reproduce. Because the whole scenario hinges on that timing, and the timing is untestable against direct evidence, the Grand Tack should be held as a strong candidate explanation rather than a settled fact, and alternative accounts of Mars's small mass remain in play.

**7 — Open problem: shield, slingshot, or neither.** **(a)** *For protection:* Jupiter clears its neighbourhood, deflecting and ejecting long-period comets and other bodies that would otherwise reach the inner planets, and it demonstrably consumes some — the Shoemaker–Levy 9 impact of 1994 being a captured comet that struck Jupiter rather than wandering the inner system. *For hazard:* Jupiter's mean-motion resonances (the Kirkwood gaps) are the principal machinery that pumps main-belt asteroids up to high eccentricity and injects them onto Earth-crossing orbits in the first place; the same gravity that can deflect can also aim. **(b)** An intermediate-mass giant is massive enough to perturb the small-body reservoirs strongly — stirring up eccentricities and flinging bodies inward — but not massive enough to *eject* that material efficiently onto escape trajectories or into the Sun. So it maximises the throughput of dangerous bodies into the inner system while doing little of the clean-up that a full-mass Jupiter accomplishes. Both very small giants (which barely stir anything) and very large ones (which eject what they stir) deliver fewer impactors than the awkward middle. **(c)** Run a suite of long-term N-body integrations of the actual solar system, identical in every respect except that Jupiter's mass is varied across a grid from zero up through and beyond its real value; populate each run with realistic reservoirs (main belt, Kuiper belt / scattered disk, Oort cloud) and integrate for several billion years, counting the number of bodies that reach Earth-crossing orbits or actually strike Earth. Compare the impact flux at the real Jupiter mass against the zero-mass and intermediate-mass cases. A clean answer would be a monotonic curve showing the real Jupiter delivers *fewer* impactors than any alternative (net-protective) or *more* than the no-giant case (net-hazard); the honest expectation, from Horner and Jones, is a non-monotonic curve on which our Jupiter is neither the best nor the worst — meaning "shield" is simply the wrong frame. **(d)** *One defensible position:* a Jupiter is closer to irrelevant-to-harmful than to prerequisite for inner-planet habitability. The exoplanet catalogue shows that compact systems of inner planets exist in abundance without any outer Jupiter analogue, so a distant giant is plainly not required to *assemble* rocky worlds; and the dynamical evidence shows a giant is at least as likely to bombard an inner planet as to guard it. What would change my mind: robust evidence that giant-driven volatile delivery is *necessary* to make an inner planet wet (so that no giant means a dry, and therefore uninhabitable, inner world), or population-level statistics showing that inner planets in systems with a well-placed giant have measurably calmer impact histories than those without. Absent that, the burden of proof lies on the claim that we owe our existence to Jupiter's guardianship — a claim the physics, as it stands, does not carry.$astroGP_master$,
    true,
    true
  )
  on conflict (node_id, depth, level, format) do update
    set body = excluded.body, generated = excluded.generated, reviewed = excluded.reviewed
    where content.body is distinct from excluded.body
       or content.generated is distinct from excluded.generated
       or content.reviewed is distinct from excluded.reviewed
  returning node_id
)
delete from content
where node_id in (select node_id from master)
  and not (depth = 'research' and level = 'advanced' and format = 'read');

with q as (
