must replace the 'eight beads on a wire' picture of the solar system. Which set of figures correctly captures its 'scale correction'?", "options": ["Jupiter holds about 50 percent of the planetary mass, Jupiter plus Saturn about 75 percent, and roughly half of all moons orbit the giants.", "Jupiter holds about 71 percent of the planetary mass, Jupiter plus Saturn about 92.5 percent, Jupiter alone outweighs all other planets combined by a factor of 2.47, and on the order of 99 percent of all moons orbit the four giants.", "The four giants together hold about 92.5 percent of the planetary mass, but it is split fairly evenly among them, and about 71 percent of moons orbit Jupiter alone.", "Jupiter holds about 92.5 percent of the planetary mass by itself, and essentially every moon in the solar system orbits it."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The numbers are the backbone of the 'scale correction' section. Total planetary mass is 446.6 Earth masses; Jupiter's 317.8 is 71.2 percent of that, and Jupiter plus Saturn (413.0) is 92.5 percent. Everything that is not Jupiter sums to 128.8 Earth masses, so Jupiter outweighs all other planets combined by 317.8/128.8 = 2.47. The moon count runs the same way: the giants command satellite systems of over a hundred (Jupiter, Saturn) and dozens (Uranus, Neptune) each, while the four terrestrials have three moons between them, so on the order of 99 percent of moons orbit a giant. The chapter stresses these are not independent facts: gravitational reach lets the same bodies seize both the gas and the retinue of moons. Option three wrongly claims the mass is split evenly among the giants (it is overwhelmingly Jupiter and Saturn); option four confuses the two-body 92.5 percent figure with Jupiter alone."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why does the chapter insist Uranus and Neptune are 'ice giants' rather than merely 'small gas giants'?", "options": ["Because they are made of frozen water on the surface, whereas Jupiter and Saturn are gaseous all the way down with no solids at all.", "Because they orbit farther from the Sun, and any planet beyond a certain distance is classified as an ice giant regardless of what it is made of.", "Because their bulk is a hot, dense mantle of ices (water, ammonia, methane) under only a thin hydrogen-helium skin, recording that their cores crossed the critical mass too late, after the gas had largely dispersed, so they never reached runaway gas accretion.", "Because they failed to form a solid core at all, and so could only trap the ices that condensed directly around them rather than accreting hydrogen."], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "Composition is a fossil of where and when a planet formed. Jupiter and Saturn have near-solar hydrogen-helium bulk because they reached the critical core mass early, while the disk was still fat with gas, and gorged in runaway accretion. Uranus (14.5 Earth masses) and Neptune (17.1) sit close to the critical core mass itself, with only one to a few Earth masses of hydrogen as a genuine atmosphere rather than the bulk. That records cores that formed farther out, where the disk was thinner and colder and orbital times longer, so growth was slow and they crossed the threshold too late, catching only a whiff of gas before the disk evaporated. 'Small gas giant' wrongly implies they are the same kind of object as Jupiter scaled down; in fact they are compositionally different (mostly ice, not mostly hydrogen) and a record of a different formation history. Option four is wrong because they did form cores; option one misdescribes both classes."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "In the Grand Tack scenario, how does Jupiter explain why Mars is only about one-ninth of Earth's mass (0.107 Earth masses)?", "options": ["Jupiter's gravity stripped away Mars's atmosphere and outer layers after it had already formed at full size, shrinking it to its present mass.", "Jupiter, still embedded in the gas disk, migrated inward to about 1.5 AU and then back out to near 5 AU, truncating the disk of solids near 1 AU, so Mars formed in a starved, swept region with little to accrete.", "Jupiter captured most of the planetesimals that would have built Mars and incorporated them into its own growing core.", "Jupiter's resonances ejected Mars from its original orbit closer to the Sun and left it stranded in a sparse outer region of the terrestrial zone."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Smooth-disk simulations that grow the terrestrials reliably overproduce Mars by a factor of order five to ten, yielding an Earth- or Venus-sized body where we find one a ninth of Earth's mass. The Grand Tack blames Jupiter: forming near 3.5 AU, it migrated inward to about 1.5 AU (roughly Mars's present orbit) and then reversed, or 'tacked,' back out to near 5 AU when Saturn caught up and locked into resonance. Acting like a snowplough, Jupiter shepherded and scattered the inner planetesimals and truncated the disk of solids at about 1 AU, leaving a sharp outer edge. Mars formed in that starved, swept region and stalled, while Earth and Venus grew to full size in the dense surviving material inside the truncation. The same passage also raked the asteroid belt, depleting its mass and mixing dry inner bodies with icy outer ones. Options that invoke atmospheric stripping, core cannibalism, or ejection misstate the mechanism, which is truncation of the solid disk."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The Kirkwood gaps are swept clean at radii where an asteroid's orbital period is a simple whole-number ratio of Jupiter's. Why does a mean-motion resonance empty a gap rather than trap objects in it?", "options": ["Jupiter's gravity is simply strongest at those radii, so it physically pulls any asteroid there out of the belt on a single close pass.", "The asteroids at those radii collide with each other more often because they are bunched together, grinding themselves to dust that radiation pressure then blows away.", "At the resonance the asteroid meets Jupiter at the same orbital phase each cycle, so Jupiter's small tugs arrive coherently and add up, pumping the asteroid's eccentricity until its orbit crosses Mars or Earth or dives toward the Sun and it is removed.", "Gas drag is enhanced at resonant radii, gradually spiraling asteroids inward until they leave the gap empty."], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "At a mean-motion resonance (3:1, 5:2, 7:3, 2:1) the asteroid and Jupiter line up at the same phase every cycle, so the little gravitational tugs come coherently and add instead of averaging away. Over thousands of orbits these accumulated kicks pump the asteroid's eccentricity higher until its stretched orbit crosses that of Mars or Earth or plunges sunward, and a close encounter or collision removes it. The gap is thus an escape hatch, not a place asteroids cannot reach. The chapter uses Kepler's third law to show the 3:1 resonance sits at 5.204 AU times (1/3)^(2/3) = 2.50 AU, exactly where the belt is empty. Crucially, the same resonant machinery can protect rather than destroy: the Trojans (1:1) and Pluto (3:2 with Neptune) librate so the body is always far from the perturber at conjunction. The sign depends on the phase relationship the resonance enforces, not on raw force strength, collisions, or gas drag."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter treats the popular claim that 'Jupiter is Earth's shield' as, at best, half true. Lay out the honest case: give the strongest argument that Jupiter protects Earth and the strongest that it is a hazard, explain the Horner and Jones finding that an intermediate-mass giant can be the worst case of all, and state the chapter's overall verdict.", "rubric": "A strong answer presents both sides using specific dynamical mechanisms from the chapter. For protection: Jupiter clears its neighbourhood, deflecting and ejecting comets and asteroids that would otherwise reach the inner planets, and it demonstrably consumes some, the cited example being Comet Shoemaker-Levy 9, whose fragments Jupiter captured, tore apart, and swallowed in July 1994, leaving Earth-sized bruises. For hazard: the same gravity that deflects also injects; Jupiter's mean-motion resonances (the very Kirkwood gaps) are the principal machinery that pumps main-belt asteroids up to high eccentricity and onto Earth-crossing orbits in the first place, and the physics does not distinguish delivering water from delivering a catastrophe. The answer should explain the Horner and Jones result: they rebuilt the solar system with giants of varying mass and counted impactors reaching Earth, finding the balance non-monotonic. Removing the giant entirely can lower the impact flux from some populations because you remove the scatterer that feeds them inward. An intermediate-mass giant (between a large ice giant and a modest gas giant) can be the worst case because it is massive enough to stir the small-body reservoirs and fling their contents inward, but not massive enough to eject that material efficiently onto escape trajectories or into the Sun, so it maximises throughput of dangerous bodies while doing little cleanup; both very small giants (which barely stir anything) and very large ones (which eject what they stir) deliver fewer impactors. The full-mass Jupiter is if anything better at ejecting than a smaller giant, but is nobody's clean guardian. The verdict: Jupiter is a shield AND a slingshot; which dominates depends on the impactor population, the giant's mass, and the epoch, the net balance is close enough to even that serious simulations disagree about its sign, and the tidy 'big friendly Jupiter' fable is not supported by the dynamics. A textbook arguing a thesis has an obligation not to pass it along uncritically."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter's thesis is that a single circumstance made the giants and then dictated everything else. Trace that argument from the snow line through to the two kinds of giant, explaining how one race, won or lost, produces both the giant/terrestrial divide and the gas-giant/ice-giant split. Use the chapter's actual numbers.", "rubric": "A strong answer reconstructs the causal chain the chapter builds. It begins with the disk temperature law T(r) = 278 K times (r/AU)^(-1/2), which places the snow line where water freezes (below about 150 to 170 K) at roughly 2.7 AU. Crossing that line, the surface density of solids jumps by a factor of two to four because water is built from hydrogen and oxygen, among the most abundant elements. Since the isolation mass scales roughly as surface density to the three-halves power, that jump (for example tripling the density) multiplies a core's reachable mass by about 3^(3/2) = 5.2, letting outer cores reach the critical core mass of about 10 Earth masses, where a core's gravity can no longer support the gas it has gathered and runaway gas accretion begins, pulling in hundreds of Earth masses of hydrogen and helium in tens of thousands to a hundred thousand years. This race is run against the disk clock: the gas disk dissipates in a few million years (two to ten, most gone by about three). Beyond the snow line cores win the race and become giants; inside it, starved of ice, they stall at terrestrial or largest-asteroid sizes. That single circumstance explains the scale correction: nearly all the planetary mass ends up outside the line, 92.5 percent in Jupiter and Saturn, 71 percent in Jupiter alone (317.8 of 446.6 Earth masses), and about 99 percent of moons orbit the giants, because gravitational reach seizes both gas and retinue. The SAME race, decided by timing, produces the gas-giant/ice-giant split: cores that reached critical mass early, while the disk was full of gas, gorged and kept near-solar hydrogen-helium (Jupiter and Saturn, gas giants); cores that reached the threshold late, after the gas had largely dispersed (farther out, where the disk was thinner and colder and orbital times longer, so growth was slower), caught only a thin hydrogen skin over an ice mantle and became ice giants (Uranus 14.5, Neptune 17.1 Earth masses, near the critical core mass itself). The strongest answers close by noting that composition is a fossil of where and when, and that the entire architecture, including the reshaping of the inner solar system, is downstream of the single snow-line-versus-disk-clock race."}]$astroGP_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
